WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.803
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.803 --> 00:00:08.809
AI is on the verge of
revolutionizing healthcare.

00:00:08.809 --> 00:00:13.691
Without even looking at you let alone
laying a hand on you I can diagnose and

00:00:13.691 --> 00:00:18.024
treat any number of medical
conditions with far greater speed and

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:20.800
accuracy than even House on his best day.

00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.311
One infamous GSB alum even went so
far to say, that you want the next

00:00:27.311 --> 00:00:32.130
generation of doctors, go look in
the computer science department.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:36.847
Algorithms will replace stethoscope,
and you know what,

00:00:36.847 --> 00:00:41.955
even if that meant that I was out
of a job, I wouldn't even care.

00:00:41.955 --> 00:00:46.976
Because all I really care about
is doing what's best for you.

00:00:46.976 --> 00:00:50.324
And when the price of human
error is just so great,

00:00:50.324 --> 00:00:55.835
I can totally understand that it might be
time to start dispensing with the human.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:02.523
Imagine that you've been awake for
52 hours, but you're not at Burning Man,

00:01:02.523 --> 00:01:04.504
you're just getting home from work.

00:01:04.504 --> 00:01:08.104
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:08.104 --> 00:01:13.340
you've only just nodded off to
sleep before the phone rings again.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:18.657
And you need to be on your game
straight away because it's Eric,

00:01:18.657 --> 00:01:23.190
a first year doctor working
alone in rural Australia.

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:27.660
And he's ringing you he says because he's
very worried about a patient he's got.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:34.240
In fact, he thinks he needs urgent surgery
because the scan, it looks kind of funny.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.219
I roll my eyes.

00:01:36.219 --> 00:01:40.686
We can't spend a $100,000 and deploy
a skilled airlift retrieval team for

00:01:40.686 --> 00:01:43.198
patient with a diagnosis
of funny I tell Eric.

00:01:43.198 --> 00:01:46.751
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:01:46.751 --> 00:01:51.820
I ask him if he can send me a snapshot
of the CT scan, which he does.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:01:55.999
And thankfully,
it looks entirely unremarkable.

00:01:55.999 --> 00:01:59.808
I ring him back, and
perhaps just because I'm so exhausted,

00:01:59.808 --> 00:02:04.130
I ignore the quiver of uncertainty in
his voice and I just say to him with

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:09.130
all the confidence in the world, there's
nothing wrong with this scan, mate.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.378
Don't send him over by air emergently
tonight, but send him over by road and

00:02:12.378 --> 00:02:14.074
I'll see him sometime in the morning.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.839
And I fall asleep before I even hang up.

00:02:20.839 --> 00:02:25.454
The next morning, refreshed after my
four-and-a-half hours, I walk into work,

00:02:25.454 --> 00:02:28.160
and I arrive at about
the same time as the patient.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:34.461
I take the scan and I load it up into
the computer and what I see is remarkable.

00:02:34.461 --> 00:02:39.592
Not only is his aorta dissected, which
means that the wall has split into two,

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:42.409
but he's actually leaking blood from it.

00:02:42.409 --> 00:02:48.100
This man needs urgent surgery, but he
needed it hours ago while I was sleeping.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:52.030
I ran over to him to try and
undo my mistake.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:57.770
In fact, I want to take a scalpel out
there and then and get going, but I can't.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.181
His name's Jack.

00:02:59.181 --> 00:03:03.623
He's got a shiny bald head
flanked by unruly gray curls.

00:03:03.623 --> 00:03:09.791
He's got this fantastic mustache sitting
on top of a very broad toothless smile.

00:03:09.791 --> 00:03:13.935
And somehow he's managing to eat peanut
M&amp;Ms which are perched on his pot belly.

00:03:13.935 --> 00:03:16.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And he's meant to be fasting but

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:19.150
I can't blame him because
they're my favorite too.

00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.680
And he-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:21.895
&gt;&gt; And he offers me one.

00:03:21.895 --> 00:03:26.370
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We shake hands and

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.780
we look each other in the eye, and
he says to me, good day, David.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.440
Boy, am I glad to see you.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.680
Eric told me that you were the man.

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:39.669
And boy, boy, I'm feeling sheepish.

00:03:39.669 --> 00:03:44.839
And I look him back in the eye and
I say, hardly the man, Jack.

00:03:44.839 --> 00:03:47.302
In fact I think I made a bit of an error.

00:03:49.365 --> 00:03:54.154
But those were the last words that Jack
heard because, and it was the funniest

00:03:54.154 --> 00:03:59.091
sensation ever, but his hand went limp
in mine, and he tried to say something,

00:03:59.091 --> 00:04:02.719
but couldn't, and
he just let off a little bit of a groan.

00:04:02.719 --> 00:04:07.060
And behind me on the screen you see his
blood-pressure go from 70 to 30 to 0.

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.769
And people run from everywhere and
pound on his chest.

00:04:12.769 --> 00:04:16.150
But I know it's futile,
because his aorta is ruptured and

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.500
there's no getting him back from that,
and it's all because of my error.

00:04:25.228 --> 00:04:30.759
The could of's and the should of's of this
scene still keep me up late at night.

00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:36.249
But it was only because of Jack that this
year a colleague of mine at Stanford and

00:04:36.249 --> 00:04:41.076
I built a deep learning algorithm
that could automatically diagnose

00:04:41.076 --> 00:04:44.919
this exact emergency and
recommend a treatment plan.

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:48.503
Jack would've been flown to hospital,
I would've been woken up, but

00:04:48.503 --> 00:04:50.189
he would've had his life saved.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.844
And this is just one example
of why when people say,

00:04:56.844 --> 00:05:00.589
AI in health care is just way
more than a buzz term to me.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:07.987
But while I'm up late at night thinking
about Jack, I also can't help but

00:05:07.987 --> 00:05:11.279
wonder what does this mean for
the future of medicine?

00:05:13.329 --> 00:05:18.432
Would it really be safest if we just
distill all of our patients, you guys,

00:05:18.432 --> 00:05:22.969
down to processes of pattern recognition,
and image detection?

00:05:26.089 --> 00:05:30.758
And if so when you or
your loved ones are at your sickest and

00:05:30.758 --> 00:05:34.473
most vulnerable who do
you want by your side?

00:05:34.473 --> 00:05:38.604
Man or machine?

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:44.248
Well, only a few years before Jack, I was
sitting on the other side of the fence,

00:05:44.248 --> 00:05:48.981
in the waiting room with the person
I loved most, my sister Jackie.

00:05:48.981 --> 00:05:54.189
Waiting for a diagnosis, and
waiting for a treatment plan.

00:05:54.189 --> 00:05:59.048
And I do wonder how that day
might've gone were we to

00:05:59.048 --> 00:06:02.785
need a machine, say an AI oncologist.

00:06:02.785 --> 00:06:08.298
Because any good AI oncologist
would need data inputs,

00:06:08.298 --> 00:06:13.224
and these were Jackie's, age 16, symptoms,

00:06:13.224 --> 00:06:18.990
intermittent pelvic pain,
loss of weight and bloating.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:24.010
Find exit operation,
a large malignant mass.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:27.556
Now, if medicine just about
finding the right diagnosis and

00:06:27.556 --> 00:06:32.005
giving the right treatment, this would
be a five minute consultation, and

00:06:32.005 --> 00:06:33.830
I could right that code today.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:39.819
Your data outputs would be,
diagnosis, a rare malignant tumor.

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:45.559
Treatment plan, twelve months of
chemotherapy and radiotherapy.

00:06:45.559 --> 00:06:49.396
Prognosis, uncertain.

00:06:49.396 --> 00:06:54.320
But surely, even a half-assed AI
oncologist would want to know if we had

00:06:54.320 --> 00:06:58.190
any questions, because that day,
Jackie had plenty.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:04.560
AI oncologist, am I going to lose my hair,
and how am I going to look?

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.199
Well, you will lose your hair,
you'll look bald.

00:07:10.656 --> 00:07:14.907
AI oncologist I love children,
I'm 16 now, am I going to be able

00:07:14.907 --> 00:07:19.265
to have children after all this
chemotherapy and radiotherapy?

00:07:22.289 --> 00:07:27.908
The statistics would suggest unlikely but
I'm not an AI gynecologist.

00:07:27.908 --> 00:07:32.499
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; AI oncologist, am I going to die?

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:39.768
And as the tears streamed down
our faces surely any half decent

00:07:39.768 --> 00:07:45.684
AI oncologist would be able to use
image detection to see those tears and

00:07:45.684 --> 00:07:49.050
robotically dispense us with a tissue.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.355
But is this really the kind
of care that we want?

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.874
Because at that moment, what those
two petrified kids needed was so

00:07:58.874 --> 00:08:02.140
much more than a diagnosis and
treatment plan.

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.639
In fact, that was the easy bit.

00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:09.389
We didn't need some Watson or advanced
clinical decision support software.

00:08:09.389 --> 00:08:14.539
What we needed and what we got thankfully,
was a very human person who would

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:19.604
stand up, walk over to the other side
of the desk, put his arms around us,

00:08:19.604 --> 00:08:24.950
and say that despite what the algorithm
said, everything was going to be okay.

00:08:24.950 --> 00:08:30.590
That he, and not some fandangled machine,
would be with us every step of the way.

00:08:33.819 --> 00:08:38.969
Yes, AI is going to do amazing things and
it's going to help all of us in this room.

00:08:38.969 --> 00:08:43.379
It's going to save lives,
it's going to slash medical error, and

00:08:43.379 --> 00:08:45.969
it's going to improve access to care.

00:08:45.969 --> 00:08:51.700
But being a great doctor is about so
much more than making the right diagnosis.

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:56.248
It requires a delicacy, a touch,
and a judgment that even the best

00:08:56.248 --> 00:08:59.590
technologists will never
be able to replicate,

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:07.719
So as we build amazing machines
to make you guys safer,

00:09:07.719 --> 00:09:13.484
it will not be the computer scientists who
become the great doctors of the future.

00:09:13.484 --> 00:09:17.839
It will be humans like Jackie,
who overcame the algorithms and

00:09:17.839 --> 00:09:20.810
just graduated medical school last year.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:27.221
Who-
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:27.221 --> 00:09:29.947
&gt;&gt; The humans like Jackie who possess

00:09:29.947 --> 00:09:34.187
the skills that is just way
too difficult to code for.

00:09:34.187 --> 00:09:37.790
[APPLAUSE].

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:42.170
[MUSIC].

